    "lawCode": "Rpld §3114, add §3422, Ins L (as proposed in S. 7658-B & A.8869-B)",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2231\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to insurance for victims\nof a hate crime; and to repeal certain provisions of such law relating\nthereto\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2022,\nc.676, to clarify provisions relating to insurance for victims of a hate\ncrime\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill prohibits insurance companies from changing policy premiums\nbecause a policyholder submitted several claims for losses resulting\nfrom a hate crime. The changes expand the list of policies covered by\nthe bill to include independently owned and operated businesses that\nemploy 100 or fewer individuals; prohibit an insurance company from\nexcluding coverage for a hate crime loss; clarify the intent behind\nprohibiting coverage cancelation since it could have been read as super-\nseding other sections of Insurance Law that deal with cancellation;\nallow the Department of Financial Services to increase premiums to\nensure solvency; and push the effective date to 90 days so insurance\ncompanies can revise their policy forms and avoid contract impairment.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nNone\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is a negotiated change to the underlying chapter.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 676 of the Laws of 2022. S.7658B and\nA.8869B both passed the Senate and Assembly in May 2022.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law and shall apply to all policies issued, renewed, modified,\naltered or amended on or after such date; provided, however, that the\nprovisions of section one of this act shall take effect on the same date\nand in the same manner as a chapter of the laws of 2022 amending the\ninsurance law relating to insurance for victims of a hate crime, as\nproposed in legislative bills numbers S. 7658-B and A.  8869B, takes\neffect.\n",
